---
import Base from '../layouts/Base.astro';
import { getDatasets, url } from '../lib/data';
const count = (await getDatasets()).length;
---
<Base title="About · AI Datasets">
  <article class="prose">
    <h1>About</h1>
    <p>
      Hugging Face lists hundreds of thousands of datasets. This site goes the other way: {count} that
      shaped AI, across vision, language models, text, tabular data, time series and audio, each shown well enough that
      you come away knowing what the data looks like.
    </p>
    <h2>How entries are chosen</h2>
    <ul>
      <li>It changed the field (ImageNet, MNIST, SQuAD, Anthropic HH-RLHF), or</li>
      <li>it was the standard benchmark for years (COCO, GLUE, MMLU, GSM8K), or</li>
      <li>it is in every introductory course (Iris, Titanic, CIFAR-10).</li>
    </ul>
    <h2>Where the samples come from</h2>
    <p>
      When a dataset is added, a script picks a fixed random sample of rows from the{' '}
      <a href="https://huggingface.co/docs/dataset-viewer">Hugging Face dataset viewer</a> and keeps it,
      so the pages do not change from one visit to the next. Where the official copy cannot be read by
      the viewer, a community copy is used; the Hugging Face button and the line under the samples name
      the exact repository and revision.
      Samples stay under each dataset's license, which is listed on its page.
      Gated benchmarks, whose authors ask that questions not be published, such as GPQA and
      Humanity's Last Exam, are linked but not sampled.
    </p>
    <h2>Getting the data</h2>
    <p>
      Every page has a "Get the data" box with a command for the Hugging Face CLI, and the same in Python
      with the <code>datasets</code> library or Polars. Check the license and size first: some datasets are
      for research use only, and some are hundreds of gigabytes.
    </p>
    <h2>For language models</h2>
    <p>
      <a href={url('/llms.txt')}><code>llms.txt</code></a> lists every dataset with a link to a plain-Markdown
      version of its page (the page URL plus <code>.md</code>), and{' '}
      <a href={url('/llms-full.txt')}><code>llms-full.txt</code></a> has all of them in one file.
    </p>
  </article>
</Base>
<style>
  .prose { max-width: 680px; padding-top: 16px; }
</style>
